european union leaders have committed to  a tougher clamp down on tax evasion and  fraud amid a growing public outcry over  doubtful practices that cost the EU  around 1 trillion euros each year at the  summit in Brussels the leaders set an  end of June deadline for new measures to  crack down on fraud on value-added tax  the speed of negotiations on tougher  savings tax agreements with Switzerland  Liechtenstein Monaco and Dora and San  Marino will be accelerated at the same  time the leaders unanimously committed  to complete by the end of the year the e  u--'s revised savings directive which  has proved a thorny dossier until now  they also agreed to push internationally  for automatic information sharing on a  wide range of taxable income an end of  year deadlines were set on taxation in  the business sector like the issue of  profit shares and aggressive tax  planning by major companies which made  headlines in recent days there's a  strong political world by the leaders  not only the Europeans but also on a  global level to go forward in attacking  tax fraud and tax evasion it is a new  situation it is unprecedented the crisis  unprecedented and the awareness on  tackling this issue is unprecedented  also high on the leaders agenda was  energy and ways to tap into sustainable  and affordable supplies the talks  focused on energy efficiency and how to  turn the e use 27 energy markets into  one energy market by next year with no  nation left unconnected by 2015 the  leaders also discussed ways to diversify  energy sources to ensure that no EU  nation has only one supplier or one  supply route they also pledged to  consult with the EU partners before  taking any action on energy that might  have a major impact on their neighbors
 

